  "dimensional_findings": {
    "immediate_literary_context": "Judah’s transformed offer in Genesis 44 precedes Joseph’s loss of composure, dismissal of attendants, self-disclosure and the brothers’ inability to answer; Genesis 44 remains contextual only.",
    "archaeology_material_culture": "The clothing, silver and provisions evidence is distinct from the wagon item; it makes no exact garment/value claim and cites no archaeological assemblage as direct explanation.",
    "historical_context": "Tell el-Dabʿa/Egypt–Levant scholarship contextualizes mobility and population presence in its own periods only; no identification of Jacob’s household, Joseph, dynasty or Hyksos shortcut.",
    "intertextual_canonical_connections": "Genesis 50:20 is an intra-Genesis reprise; Psalm 105:16–22 is later canonical reception/context only. Neither is an applicability anchor."
  },
  "providence_safeguard": "The brothers’ sale of Joseph remains culpable wrongdoing. Genesis 45’s divine preservation interpretation and Genesis 50:20’s later reprise hold human evil intent and divine good purpose together, without moral neutrality, fatalism or a complete philosophical system.",
